    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. It seems like a lot of this \"reshoring\" stuff is basically just nations acting in their own best interest to protect their workers and stability. Is it generally seen as a regressive step for the global economy, or is it just a necessary correction to the way things were running before?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Whether reshoring is regressive or a necessary correction is hotly debated among economists. Critics argue it fragments the global economy, leads to higher consumer prices, and reduces efficiency gains from specialization. However, proponents view it as a necessary recalibration to build resilience against future crises, such as pandemics or geopolitical conflicts, after decades of relying on lean, hyper-efficient global supply chains. It represents a shift from pure efficiency to incorporating security and sovereignty as key trade priorities.",
      "source": "target",
