    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I get that it's debated, but doesn't it feel like the last few decades of hyper-globalization basically ignored the stability of individual nations just to save a few bucks for big corporations? It feels like the \"efficiency\" argument is often used to dismiss the very real damage done to local manufacturing and national control. Do you think the pendulum is fundamentally swinging back in the right direction now?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many observers agree that the pursuit of unchecked efficiency often came at the expense of local stability and national resilience. The pendulum is indeed swinging away from pure globalization toward a model that balances trade with national security, which many see as a more sustainable approach. While some argue the process is clumsy, this shift reflects a growing societal demand to prioritize economic sovereignty alongside lower costs. Therefore, the direction hints at a necessary, though uneven, correction toward more managed trade.",
      "source": "target",
